@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@ a later package, that a reviewer may want to change.
 | R12 | **Reserved entries.** Each entry this package keeps is read only under its own kind and fails closed otherwise (no backup, restricted, no card). The vault process's `/credential` write accepts only a built-in adapter's credential name and never replaces an entry of another kind. | CRED-1, REC-1, REC-2 | — |
 | R13 | This package imports the vault and runs in the vault process; the vault's importer list gains `recovery` and `cmd/agentos-a8scan`, and recovery's own imports test lets only those two import it. | ARC-1, CRED-1 | — |
 | R14 | **The rotation's card lives on `card.Display`, not on `Pending` (CTRL-card-rot).** `Pending` holds the parts, the Wi-Fi name, the proof and the deadline, never a card: the caller makes a `card.Display` with `Rotate: p.Parts()` and `WiFiName: p.WiFiName()`, whose `Provision` calls `Commit(b, next, now)` with the confirmed card. `Commit` checks that `next` carries exactly the pending parts (`ErrNextCard`) and returns `ErrPendingLapsed` or `ErrCardNotStored`; the caller's provisioner, not `Commit` (recovery cannot import `card`, which pulls in `net/http`), maps a lapse to the display's `ErrDiscard` and a partial write to `card.ErrInEffect` (release CTRL-card-rot-wire R1). After a partial write the owner has confirmed from their copy and `Pending` is used up, so the display erases its copy, refuses cancel and names the step that finishes: keep both cards and start a new rotation with whichever card's passphrase or recovery key works. Once a slot is about to be written, `Commit` marks every rotated part owed, not only the slots: the Wi-Fi password, setup secret and grid are stored after the slots, so after a partial write they are still the old card's, and a narrower finishing rotation would leave the new card's Wi-Fi password never in effect (#1090 review F3). The next rotation must replace every owed part (a Wi-Fi and recovery rotation that crashed after the recovery slot reads `[wifi recovery refresh]`, since Refresh does not finish the recovery part), and its proof is whichever factor opens the drive: the new key after the recovery slot was written, the old key if the passphrase slot was written and the recovery write failed. Cancel is `Display.Discard` then `Pending.Cancel`; nothing is written before the confirm, so an abort, a lapse or a restart keeps the old key. A test pins that `Pending` has no display of its own (no `Card`/`Prompt`/`Confirm`/`Answer` method, no `ErrConfirm`). | REC-4, HW-1 | Wiring into the vault process and agentosd: release CTRL-card-rot-wire. |
+| R15 | **The card and its parts are stored in one vault write** (`vault.PutMany`, #1440). The redactor matches whole entry values, so the Wi-Fi password and setup secret are their own entries beside the card (R9); separate writes would let a failure or crash between them leave a card whose secret no entry matches. `storeCard` (provisioning and rotation) puts all three in one save: the vault holds all of them or none, in memory and on the drive. Tests fail the rollback counter at every save boundary of a commit and of provisioning and check, in memory and after reopen, that the card in the vault is fully covered by the redactor. | CRED-7, REC-4 | — |

